<Base title="The Bunjevci"
  eyebrow="Why these villages exist at all"
  dek="Smokvica Krmpotska and Krivi Put are not ancient. They were settled on purpose, in living memory of the Ottoman wars, by refugees the Habsburgs planted on an empty mountain to hold it."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Two of Hedviga's four grandparental lines — the <strong>Blaževići</strong> of
    Smokvica Krmpotska and the <strong>Prpići</strong> of Krivi Put — come out of villages in the
    Senj hinterland that <strong>did not exist in 1600</strong>. Knowing that changes what it means
    to say the family is “from” there.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Who the Bunjevci are</h2>
    <p>The <strong>Bunjevci</strong> are a Catholic South Slavic group whose homeland before the
    Ottoman wars lay in the western Balkan hinterland, around the Krmpote district near
    <strong>Zemunik</strong> behind Zadar. The <strong>Krmpotić</strong> clan — the largest of
    them — abandoned that territory under Ottoman pressure between about
    <strong>1465 and 1467</strong>, and moved north and toward the coast.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">They are usually described in three branches: the
    <strong>Dalmatian</strong> Bunjevci, the <strong>Danubian</strong> Bunjevci who ended up around
    Subotica in the Bačka, and the <strong>Littoral–Lika</strong> Bunjevci. This family belongs to
    the third.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1605–1647</div>
    <h2>The planting of the Senj mountain</h2>
    <p>The first organised migration to the Croatian Littoral is dated to <strong>1605</strong>,
    when about <strong>fifty families</strong> from Krmpote near Zemunik were settled at
    <strong>Lič</strong> near Fužine. Further waves down to <strong>1647</strong> settled the
    hinterland of Senj — <strong>Ledenice, Krmpote, Krivi Put</strong> and the
    <strong>Senjska draga</strong>. The settlers brought the name of the district they had left:
    the new <em>Krmpote</em> in the Vinodol hills is named for the old Krmpote behind Zadar, and
    <strong>Smokvica Krmpotska</strong> — “Smokvica of the Krmpote people” — carries it in its
    surname.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">This was frontier policy, not accident. The Habsburg
    <strong>Military Frontier</strong> needed a loyal, armed, Catholic population on the empty
    ground behind Senj. The settlers got land and grazing; the empire got a militia.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>The name of the road.</strong> The story told locally is that a Bunjevac, asked which
      way some Venetian fugitives had gone, answered that they had taken the
      <em>crooked path</em> — <strong><em>krivi put</em></strong> — and the district, until then
      called <em>svetojakovski</em> and then <em>krmpotski</em>, has been Krivi Put ever since.
      This archive records it as <strong>local tradition</strong>, which is what it is.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What that means for this family</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Krivi Put 136</h3>
        <p>The Prpići are recorded at a specific house: <strong class="mono">Krivi Put 136</strong>.
        <strong>Thirty-five people</strong> in this tree are born at that address, including
        <a href={u("/people/antonija-tonka-perpic-prpic/")}>Antonija “Tonka” Prpić</a> on
        <strong>12 May 1859</strong>, baptised three days later at Krivi Put with
        <strong>Lucas and Anna Tomljanović</strong> standing as godparents.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">House numbers are how the Frontier registers worked. A settled
        family <em>is</em> its house number for a century at a time — which is why the same address
        can produce four generations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Smokvica Krmpotska, house 114</h3>
        <p><a href={u("/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic/")}>Toma Blažević</a> was born on
        <strong>27 January 1838</strong> at <strong class="mono">Br. 114, Smokvica
        Krmpotska</strong> — <em>br.</em> for <em>broj</em>, number. His father
        <a href={u("/people/anton-blazevic/")}>Anton</a>, his grandfather
        <a href={u("/people/ilija-blazevic/")}>Ilija</a> (b. <em>c.</em> 1790) and the two men above
        them are all placed in the same village.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">In <strong>1835</strong> Krivi Put was the seat of a captain's
        post under Krmpote, with <strong>98 houses and 1,246 people</strong>. That is the scale of
        the world these families were born into.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">How these households actually worked</div>
    <h2>The zadruga at Smokvica 114</h2>
    <p class="lede">The registers do not just place this family in a Bunjevac village. They show it
    living in the <strong>characteristic household form</strong> of the Frontier and the
    settlements — and this archive only saw it after reading a document meant as a control.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>1838</th><td><a href={u("/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic/")}>Toma Blažević</a> born at
        <strong><em>Br. 114, Smokvica Krmpotska</em></strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>1882, 1884</th><td><strong>Toma and Terezija Pećanić</strong>, <em>seljaci</em>, at
        Smokvica 114 — the baptisms of Ivan and Tonka</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1890</th><td><strong>Pavao Blažević and Marija Tomljanović</strong> at Smokvica 114 —
        and this Pavao is the son of <strong>Luka</strong>, not of Toma</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1895</th><td><strong>“Jurko Blažević i Tereza rođ. Žubrinić, <em>selj.</em>”</strong>
        at Smokvica kbr. 114 — the baptism of Marijan</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1912</th><td>Antonija's marriage names her parents as <strong>“†† Toma i Tereza
        Blažević, poljodjelci iz sela Smokvica Kbr. 114”</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Luka and Anton — Toma's father — are both sons of</strong>
    <a href={u("/people/ilija-blazevic/")}><strong>Ilija Blažević</strong></a>, born about 1790. So
    Pavao and Toma are <strong>first cousins</strong>, and three generations of Ilija's descendants
    share one house number.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">That is a <strong><em>zadruga</em></strong>: the South Slav extended-family household, in
    which married brothers and cousins live under one roof and one number and hold the land in
    common. It was the ordinary form of tenure in the <strong>Military Frontier</strong>, where a
    household — not a man — owed the regiment its service, and it survived in these settlements long
    after the Frontier itself was abolished in 1881.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>What it cost this archive.</strong> The house number was read here as
    a household, and used as strong evidence that Toma was Juraj's father. It is not. It identifies
    a <strong>kin-group</strong>, and cannot say which man in it was whose father.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What still could →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And why it is invisible ten miles away</div>
    <h2>The town does not count houses</h2>
    <p class="lede">The village registers of <strong>Krmpote</strong> and <strong>Krivi Put</strong>
    enter a house number against every child — <em>Smokvica 114</em>, <em>Krivi Put 136</em>,
    <em>Klenovica 22</em>, <em>Povile 22</em>. The town register of <strong>Senj</strong> does
    not.</p>
    <p>Under the same printed column — <em>mjesto obitavanja i <strong>kućni broj</strong></em>,
    place of residence <strong>and house number</strong> — a Senj entry says simply
    <strong>“Senj”</strong>. So the <a href={u("/papic/")}>Papići</a> cannot be traced through a
    house the way the Blaževići can, and a zadruga among them would be
    <strong>invisible even if it existed</strong>. That is a difference between a town and a
    village, not between two families — and it is worth knowing before concluding anything from the
    absence.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:0"><strong>The Bunjevac identity is asserted here by
    place, not by document.</strong> No record in this tree calls anyone a Bunjevac. What the
    records give is birth in villages that were founded by Bunjevci and populated by them, bearing
    surnames — Blažević, Prpić, Tomljanović, Vukelić — that are standard in those settlements. That
    is a strong inference and it is offered as an inference.
    <a href={u("/method/")}>Method →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
